{"points":[{"ruleId":"V-04","type":"ambiguous","paragraphs":["p80"],"explanation":"The acceptable tier ('No settlement admitting fault on the Customer's behalf or imposing obligations on the Customer without its consent, not unreasonably withheld') can be read two ways: as requiring consent to be qualified by 'not unreasonably withheld', or as merely describing the existing arrangement. p80 (CSA default 9.3) gives the Protected Party an unqualified consent right over fault-admitting settlements, with no reasonableness qualifier, so the clean text fails one reading and passes the other. The escalation tiers cover only exclusions, Customer-spec indemnity and the exclusive-remedy wording — nothing addresses settlement consent — so an unqualified consent right cannot be classified.","suggestion":"State expressly whether the CSA 9.3 draft (consent limited to fault-admitting or materially adverse settlements, but absolute) meets the acceptable tier; if not, move unqualified consent rights into escalation."},{"ruleId":"V-06","type":"ambiguous","paragraphs":["p32"],"explanation":"The same amendment can fall in both tiers. A standard customer ask — 'Customer Content must not be used to train any AI/ML models, including third-party models incorporated in the Product' — matches the acceptable tier ('No training on Customer Content') but also arguably matches the escalation tier ('a prohibition that reaches third-party components we rely on'), since p32 expressly covers third-party components. Striking only 'including third-party components of the Product' cannot be classified either way, and no tie-breaker is given between the acceptable and escalation readings.","suggestion":"Give a criterion: escalate only where the ban would bar use of a relied-on third-party component outright (even when no Customer Content is involved with training), not where it merely excludes Customer Content from such components' training."},{"ruleId":"V-11","type":"ambiguous","paragraphs":["p19"],"explanation":"The acceptable tier allows 'increases capped at 5%, or at CPI if higher', but the escalation tier escalates 'a cap below CPI' with no CPI reference point or date. A fixed 5% cap (as at p19) is simultaneously 'capped at 5%' (acceptable) and, whenever measured CPI exceeds 5%, plausibly 'a cap below CPI' (escalation) — the tiers collide and the reviewer needs an external, time-varying number they cannot know. Separately, the preferred requires increases 'on notice before the renewal date', but p19 contains no notice mechanism for increases, which no tier addresses.","suggestion":"Anchor the CPI test (e.g., escalate only a fixed cap below a stated percentage floor, or only where no CPI fallback is offered at renewal), and state whether the absence of an increase-notice mechanism is acceptable."},{"ruleId":"V-13","type":"ambiguous","paragraphs":["p57"],"explanation":"The acceptable tier stops at 'Deletion within 30 days' and the escalation tier starts at 'Immediate deletion', so fixed windows of 1–29 days (e.g., 7 or 14 days) fall in no bucket. V-24, which covers the same subject matter for Personal Data, escalates any fixed deletion commitment under 30 days, so a single exit-deletion regime covering both Customer Content and Personal Data (a common customer ask) is 'unclassified' under V-13 but 'escalate' under V-24 depending on which paragraph it amends. p57 itself sits at the preferred (60 days), so the ambiguity only bites on departures — but that is exactly where the reviewer must act.","suggestion":"Set one shared threshold across V-13 and V-24 (e.g., anything under 30 days escalates unless backups are unaffected by the rotation) so the same exit-deletion departure cannot be classified differently under the two rules."},{"ruleId":"V-15","type":"not-met","paragraphs":["p95","p129"],"explanation":"The preferred requires that operational policies (security, acceptable use, support) 'may be updated on notice without material reduction in protection'. The bundle grants no such right anywhere: p95 confines all changes to signed writing, and p129 only sweeps in policies 'referenced in or attached to the Key Terms', of which none are referenced. The escalation trigger ('removal of our right to update operational policies') can therefore never fire against this agreement, and the acceptable tier (30 days' notice with objection right) has no object either — the preferred/acceptable/escalation split is half inapplicable to this bundle, so the provenance claim that the clean copy meets every preferred position does not hold for this rule.","suggestion":"Either add the update-operational-policies-on-notice right to the template documents, or strip that element from the preferred so the rule tests only the signed-writing requirement it can actually measure (p95)."},{"ruleId":"V-19","type":"inconsistent","paragraphs":["a49","a50"],"explanation":"V-19 and V-23 both govern audits of us and contradict each other. V-19's usage notes say we accept audits only through our published security reports, and its escalation targets 'a Customer right to audit our systems or premises'; V-23's acceptable tier expressly allows one on-site audit a year at the Customer's cost, and its preferred blesses the channelling mechanism in DPA 5.1 — whose opening sentence (a49) still says Provider 'will allow for and contribute to audits, including inspections by Customer'. The same audit markup — and even the bundled a49 wording on a literal reading — can be classified as escalation under V-19 and as preferred or acceptable under V-23; neither rule says which of them owns DPA Section 5.","suggestion":"Scope V-19 to usage/licence verification under the CSA only, and defer all data-protection audits (DPA Section 5, a49–a51) to V-23; optionally add a note that the channelling sentence in 5.1 rescues the 'inspections' wording."},{"ruleId":"V-21","type":"ambiguous","paragraphs":["a24"],"explanation":"The boundary between the acceptable tier ('a right for the Customer to terminate the affected service, without penalty and with a prorated refund, if an objection cannot be resolved') and the escalation tier ('a Customer veto') is unclear for the common middle-ground formulations customers draft, e.g. 'Provider will not engage the new subprocessor until the objection is resolved; if unresolved after 60 days, Customer may terminate the affected Order Form'. Blocking use pending resolution is functionally close to a veto (escalation), while an exit with refund is acceptable — and it is also unclear whether 'the affected service' includes the whole Order Form.","suggestion":"Define the line: acceptable = an exit right limited to the affected service with refund; escalation = any mechanism that prevents Provider from engaging the subprocessor outright, even temporarily pending resolution; and state whether Order Form-wide termination counts as 'the affected service'."},{"ruleId":"V-22","type":"inconsistent","paragraphs":["a26"],"explanation":"The escalation list includes 'a duty to give the Customer copies of subprocessor contracts', yet the preferred's own source is DPA Standard Terms sections 2.6(b)–(d) — and 2.6(c), present as a26, imposes exactly that duty: Provider will share, at Customer's request, a copy of its Subprocessor agreements (redact able for business secrets). As written, V-22 escalates the very default the preferred adopts, so a reviewer applying the rule to the clean bundle would raise an escalation on a clause the playbook treats as standard, i.e. both positions fire on the same clause with opposite results.","suggestion":"Narrow the escalation (e.g., copies demanded without the 2.6(c) redaction safeguard, or flow-down obligations materially weaker than equivalent protection) and treat the standard redacted-copy sharing within the acceptable/preferred band."},{"ruleId":"V-25","type":"ambiguous","paragraphs":["a63"],"explanation":"a63 (standard DPA 8.3) says the DPA 'does not limit any liability between the parties for violations of the EEA SCCs or UK Addendum'. Read with a61 (DPA liability subject to the Agreement's waivers, exclusions and limitations) and a65 (SCCs/UK Addendum prevail over both DPA and Agreement), it cannot be decided from the rule whether SCC or Addendum-violation liability stays inside the Agreement's caps or sits outside them — which is precisely V-25's escalation test ('DPA liability outside the Agreement's limits') and arguably its hard line ('uncapped liability under the DPA beyond what Applicable Data Protection Laws require'), since whether the carve-out is itself required by law is the unsettled question. Reviewers can therefore false-positive on the clean Standard Terms.","suggestion":"State expressly that the standard 8.3 carve-out (a63) is within the preferred/acceptable band (or, alternatively, require SCC/Addendum-violation liability to remain subject to Section 8 to the extent permitted by law) so the clean terms are not misclassified as escalation."},{"ruleId":"V-27","type":"ambiguous","paragraphs":["a110"],"explanation":"The acceptable tier fixes termination right at failures 'in 3 of any 6 months' and the escalation tier only names 'a single month's failure'. Triggers between the two positions — e.g. 2 out of any 6 consecutive months, or 3 out of any 9 — are in neither list, so a customer counter between the playbook's positions cannot be classified and the reviewer is left with escalation-by-caution as the only resolution. (The clean a110 sits at the preferred, so the ambiguity bites once the customer counters.)","suggestion":"Define the boundary parametrically, e.g. escalate any termination trigger that requires fewer than 2 failures or operates over a window shorter than 3 months — whichever line is intended — instead of naming only the extreme poles."}]}
